00:58:51,428 --> 00:58:57,428
is a 2008 documentary film about the Australian New Wave
of 1970s and 1980s low-budget cinema.

13
01:03:45,714 --> 01:03:51,714
The film was written and directed by Mark Hartley, who interviewed over eighty Australian, American and British actors, directors, screenwriters and producers, including
Quentin Tarantino, Brian Trenchard-Smith, Jamie Lee Curtis, Dennis Hopper, George Lazenby, George Miller, Barry Humphries, Stacy Keach, Glory Annen, John D.

14
01:08:40,000 --> 01:08:46,000
Lamond, John Seale and Roger Ward.

15
01:13:34,285 --> 01:13:40,285
Hartley spent several years writing a detailed research document, which served to some
degree as a script for the film, about the New Wave era of Australian cinema.

16
01:18:28,571 --> 01:18:34,571
It focused on the commonly overlooked "Ozploitation" films—mainly filled with sex, horror and violence—which critics and
film historians considered vulgar and offensive, often excluded from Australia's "official film history".

17
01:23:22,857 --> 01:23:28,857
Hartley approached Quentin Tarantino, a longtime "Ozploitation" fan who had dedicated his 2003 film Kill
Bill to the exploitation genre, and Tarantino agreed to help get the project off the ground.

18
01:28:17,142 --> 01:28:23,142
Hartley then spent an additional five years interviewing subjects and editing the combined
250 hours of interviews and original stock footage into a 100-minute film.

19
01:33:11,428 --> 01:33:17,428
Not Quite Hollywood, which premiered at the 2008 Melbourne International Film Festival, did not perform well at the box office upon its Australia-wide release,
but garnered universally positive reviews from critics and a nomination for "Best Documentary" at the 2008 Australian Film Institute Awards.

20
01:38:05,714 --> 01:38:11,714
The tone is lighthearted.
